ADHD: 

Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, a mental health disorder that includes a combination of persistent problems. 

Start from childhood and continue into adulthood. 

Different types of ADHD 

ADHD is separate into three different types: 

    Inattentive type 

    Hyperactive/Impulsive type 

    Combined type 

 

Symptoms that may occor: 

The way an induvial expercies ADHD verses from severity. Not everyone with ADHD has all the symptoms. 

But here is a list of symptoms that may occur 

A kid with inattentive type may; 

    Be easily distracted  

    Having trouble following directions or finishing tasks 

    Have trouble paying attention  

    Make careless mistakes 

    Forget things easily 

    Have problems organizing daily tasks 

    Have trouble sitting still 

    Often lose things 

    Daydream a lot 

 

A kid with Hyperactive-impulsive type may; 

    Often squirm, fidget, or bounce when sitting 

    Have trouble staying seated 

    Not be able to play quietly by themselves 

    Always be moving, such as running or climbing on things 

    Talk excessively 

    Have trouble waiting for their turn 

    Blurt out answers 

    Interrupt others 

 

Combined is the combination of both Inattentive and Hyperactive-Impulsive type. 

 

Causes: 

It’s not fully known what causes ADHD, but research has shown that it’s strongly linked to genetics. 

    Environment: Certain environmental factors also may increase risk, exp lead exposure as a child. 

    Problems during development: Problems with the central nervous system at key moments in development may play a role. 

Diagnosis: 

ADHD diagnosed only when symptoms are severe enough to cause ongoing problems in more than one area of your life. The symptoms can be traced back to early childhood. 

 